ints":["Shores up the e6 pawn, which was under as much attack as it had defence (2 against 1).","The next best move, 39…Kg6, would drop Black's winning chances from 64% to 50% (0.00).","39…Qe5 stops Bxe6+.","White was threatening Bxe6+, winning a pawn. Qe5 takes care of it.","White answers 40.Bxa6, winning a pawn.","After 40.Bxa6 Qe4+ 41.Qxe4 Nxe4 42.Bd3 Nd2, the queens come off and Black is a pawn down (−1.59)."]},{"ply":103,"name":"52.Kh2","cls":"blunder","color":"w","headline":"52.Kh2 is a blunder: White's winning chances go from 45% to 3%.","points":["Black answers 52…Qxf2+, which takes the pawn on f2.","After Qxf2+, Black wins 1 point of material within 2 moves.","Better was 52.Qb8 (−0.56)."],"better":"52.Qb8 threatens Qh8+, winning a pawn, picking it up with 53.Qxh7+. After 52…Qxf2+ 53.Kh1 fxe4 54.Qf8+ Kg6 55.Qg8+, White is a piece and a pawn down (−0.56)."}],"opening":{"name":"Catalan Opening: Open Defense, Classical Line","eco":"E05","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 10…Rd8: 20 moves in all, 10 by White and 10 by Black.","White left the book with 11.Qc2. In 64 games from this position, strong players played 11.e3 (98%).","The main line goes on 11.e3 Qxc4 12.Nxc4 a5.","From here White scored 63% in those games, with 11% drawn."]}}